{"name":"Tamale Maize","hex":"#f8e7b7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tamale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Mexican dish of cornmeal dough shell filled with various ingredients (e.g. chopped beef, pork, sweet filling) then steamed in corn husks."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tamale"},{"term":"maize","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Corn; a type of grain of the species Zea mays."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/maize"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tamale","blocks":[{"text":"A tamale is a traditional Mesoamerican dish made of masa, a dough made from nixtamalized corn, which is steamed in a corn husk or banana leaves. The wrapping can either be discarded prior to eating or used as a plate. Tamales can be filled with meats, cheeses, fruits, vegetables, herbs, chilies, or any preparation according to taste, and both the filling and the cooking liquid may be seasoned."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Tamal_oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg/330px-Tamal_oaxaque%C3%B1o.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamale","wikidata":"Q1147853"},{"title":"Maize","blocks":[{"text":"Maize, also known as corn, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize","wikidata":"Q11575"},{"title":"Maize (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The shade maize or corn refers to a specific tone of yellow; it is named for the cereal of the same name—maize. In public usage, maize can be applied to a variety of shades, ranging from light yellow to a dark shade that borders on orange, since the color of maize may vary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Cornheap.jpg/330px-Cornheap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize_(color)","wikidata":"Q3843568"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Tamales-florentine-codex.png/330px-Tamales-florentine-codex.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Tamales-florentine-codex.png/330px-Tamales-florentine-codex.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tamales served to honor the birth of a child. (Florentine Codex)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATamales-florentine-codex.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/091225_tamales.JPG/500px-091225_tamales.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/091225_tamales.JPG/500px-091225_tamales.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black and red tamales in Guatemala","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A091225_tamales.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Nacatamal_with_banana_leaf_and_aluminum_foil.jpg/500px-Nacatamal_with_banana_leaf_and_aluminum_foil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 